</section>

<!-- ══════════════ 11. EXAMPLES ══════════════ -->
<section class="sec" id="sec-examples">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">11</span><h2>Worked Examples</h2></div>

  <div class="example">
    <div class="ex-h"><span>Example 1</span><h4>Infinite Slope, No Seepage</h4></div>
    <p class="prob"><strong>Problem:</strong> A long uniform hillside has &beta; = 22&deg;, with a potential failure plane 4 m down on stronger rock. Soil: c' = 12 kPa, &phi;' = 28&deg;, &gamma; = 18 kN/m&sup3;. Find FS.</p>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step"><b>1</b><div>\( \cos^2 22^\circ = 0.860,\ \tan 22^\circ = 0.404,\ \tan 28^\circ = 0.532 \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>2</b><div><strong>Cohesion term:</strong> \( \dfrac&#123;c'&#125;&#123;\gamma H\cos^2\beta\tan\beta&#125; = \dfrac&#123;12&#125;&#123;18(4)(0.860)(0.404)&#125; = \dfrac&#123;12&#125;&#123;25.0&#125; = 0.480 \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>3</b><div><strong>Friction term:</strong> \( \dfrac&#123;\tan\phi'&#125;&#123;\tan\beta&#125; = \dfrac&#123;0.532&#125;&#123;0.404&#125; = 1.316 \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>4</b><div>\( FS = 0.480 + 1.316 = 1.80 \)</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> FS = 1.80 &mdash; comfortably stable. Note that friction supplies 73% of the resistance.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="example">
    <div class="ex-h"><span>Example 2</span><h4>The Same Slope After Heavy Rain</h4></div>
    <p class="prob"><strong>Problem:</strong> The slope of Example 1 becomes fully saturated with seepage parallel to the surface. &gamma;<sub>sat</sub> = 20 kN/m&sup3;. Nothing else changes. Find the new FS.</p>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step"><b>1</b><div><strong>Submerged unit weight:</strong> \( \gamma' = 20 - 9.81 = 10.19\text&#123;kN/m&#125;^3 \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>2</b><div><strong>Cohesion term</strong> (barely changes &mdash; only &gamma; &rarr; &gamma;<sub>sat</sub>): \( \dfrac&#123;12&#125;&#123;20(4)(0.860)(0.404)&#125; = 0.432 \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>3</b><div><strong>Friction term</strong> &mdash; here is the damage: \( \dfrac&#123;\gamma'&#125;&#123;\gamma_&#123;sat&#125;&#125;\cdot\dfrac&#123;\tan\phi'&#125;&#123;\tan\beta&#125; = \dfrac&#123;10.19&#125;&#123;20&#125;(1.316) = 0.510 \times 1.316 = 0.671 \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>4</b><div>\( FS = 0.432 + 0.671 = 1.10 \)</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> FS falls from <strong>1.80 to 1.10</strong> &mdash; a 39% loss, with the friction contribution nearly halved (1.316 &rarr; 0.671). The soil is identical; only the water changed. This single comparison is the most important thing in the chapter.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="example">
    <div class="ex-h"><span>Example 3</span><h4>Taylor's Stability Number</h4></div>
    <p class="prob"><strong>Problem:</strong> A cut is planned in saturated clay under undrained conditions (&phi; = 0), c<sub>u</sub> = 30 kPa, &gamma; = 18 kN/m&sup3;, &beta; = 45&deg;. Taylor's chart gives m &asymp; 0.17. Find the critical height, and FS for a 6 m cut.</p>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step"><b>1</b><div><strong>Critical height:</strong> \( H_&#123;cr&#125; = \dfrac&#123;c_u&#125;&#123;\gamma m&#125; = \dfrac&#123;30&#125;&#123;18(0.17)&#125; = \dfrac&#123;30&#125;&#123;3.06&#125; = 9.80\text&#123;m&#125; \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>2</b><div><strong>Mobilized cohesion at H = 6 m:</strong> \( c_d = \gamma H m = 18(6)(0.17) = 18.4\text&#123;kPa&#125; \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>3</b><div><strong>Factor of safety:</strong> \( FS = \dfrac&#123;c_u&#125;&#123;c_d&#125; = \dfrac&#123;30&#125;&#123;18.4&#125; = 1.63 \)</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> H<sub>cr</sub> = 9.8 m; a 6 m cut has FS = 1.63. Because &phi; = 0, FS also equals H<sub>cr</sub>/H = 9.8/6 = 1.63.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="example">
    <div class="ex-h"><span>Example 4</span><h4>Method of Slices &mdash; OMS then Bishop</h4></div>
    <p class="prob"><strong>Problem:</strong> A trial circle is divided into 5 slices, each b = 2.0 m wide (so &ell; = b/cos&alpha;). Soil: c' = 20 kPa, &phi;' = 20&deg; (tan&phi;' = 0.364). Compute FS by the Ordinary Method, then refine with Bishop's.</p>
    <div style="overflow-x:auto;margin:.6rem 0">
      <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.78rem">
        <tr style="color:var(--mut)"><th style="text-align:left;padding:.25rem .4rem">Slice</th><th style="text-align:right;padding:.25rem .4rem">W (kN/m)</th><th style="text-align:right;padding:.25rem .4rem">&alpha; (&deg;)</th><th style="text-align:right;padding:.25rem .4rem">&ell; (m)</th><th style="text-align:right;padding:.25rem .4rem">u (kPa)</th><th style="text-align:right;padding:.25rem .4rem">W sin&alpha;</th><th style="text-align:right;padding:.25rem .4rem">OMS resist.</th></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:.25rem .4rem">1</td><td style="text-align:right">60</td><td style="text-align:right">&minus;12</td><td style="text-align:right">2.045</td><td style="text-align:right">10</td><td style="text-align:right">&minus;12.47</td><td style="text-align:right">54.82</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:.25rem .4rem">2</td><td style="text-align:right">140</td><td style="text-align:right">2</td><td style="text-align:right">2.001</td><td style="text-align:right">22</td><td style="text-align:right">4.89</td><td style="text-align:right">74.93</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:.25rem .4rem">3</td><td style="text-align:right">180</td><td style="text-align:right">16</td><td style="text-align:right">2.081</td><td style="text-align:right">28</td><td style="text-align:right">49.61</td><td style="text-align:right">83.39</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:.25rem .4rem">4</td><td style="text-align:right">150</td><td style="text-align:right">31</td><td style="text-align:right">2.333</td><td style="text-align:right">24</td><td style="text-align:right">77.25</td><td style="text-align:right">73.08</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:.25rem .4rem">5</td><td style="text-align:right">70</td><td style="text-align:right">47</td><td style="text-align:right">2.933</td><td style="text-align:right">12</td><td style="text-align:right">51.20</td><td style="text-align:right">63.22</td></tr>
        <tr style="border-top:1px solid var(--bdr);color:var(--txt)"><td style="padding:.25rem .4rem"><strong>&Sigma;</strong></td><td colspan="4"></td><td style="text-align:right"><strong>170.48</strong></td><td style="text-align:right"><strong>349.44</strong></td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step"><b>1</b><div><strong>Sample slice 3:</strong> \( c'\ell = 20(2.081) = 41.6 \); \( W\cos\alpha = 180(0.961) = 173.0 \); \( u\ell = 28(2.081) = 58.3 \). Resisting \( = 41.6 + (173.0-58.3)(0.364) = 41.6 + 41.8 = 83.4 \). Driving \( = 180\sin16^\circ = 49.6 \).</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>2</b><div><strong>OMS result:</strong> \( FS = \dfrac&#123;349.44&#125;&#123;170.48&#125; = 2.05 \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>3</b><div><strong>Bishop, round 1</strong> (start from FS = 2.05). For slice 3: \( m_\alpha = \cos16^\circ + \dfrac&#123;\sin16^\circ(0.364)&#125;&#123;2.05&#125; = 0.961 + 0.049 = 1.010 \). Numerator \( = 20(2.0) + (180-28(2.0))(0.364) = 40.0 + 45.1 = 85.1 \), giving \( 85.1/1.010 = 84.3 \). Summing all slices: \( \sum = 367.97 \), so \( FS = 367.97/170.48 = 2.16 \).</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>4</b><div><strong>Bishop, round 2</strong> (FS = 2.16): \( \sum = 369.06 \), \( FS = 2.165 \). Round 3 changes it by under 0.01 &mdash; converged.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> OMS gives FS = 2.05; Bishop converges to FS = 2.17. The Ordinary Method is 5% conservative here, exactly as expected &mdash; and note Bishop needed only two rounds to settle.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════ 12. CASE STUDY ══════════════ -->
<section class="sec" id="sec-case">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">12</span><h2>Case Study &mdash; Chattogram, 12 June 2017</h2></div>

  <p>343 mm of rain fell in one day on Rangamati, Bandarban and Chattogram. Landslides killed 152 people, severed the Chittagong&ndash;Rangamati highway, and overturned valley-side retaining walls when their foundations lost support. Islam, Islam &amp; Jeet (2021) collected undisturbed samples from six of the failed slopes and back-analysed them. Here is what the soil actually was:</p>

  <div style="overflow-x:auto;margin:1rem 0">
    <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.78rem">
      <tr style="color:var(--mut)">
        <th style="text-align:left;padding:.3rem .4rem">Site</th><th style="text-align:right;padding:.3rem .4rem">H (m)</th><th style="text-align:right;padding:.3rem .4rem">&beta;</th>
        <th style="text-align:right;padding:.3rem .4rem">&gamma; (kN/m&sup3;)</th><th style="text-align:right;padding:.3rem .4rem">c (kPa)</th><th style="text-align:right;padding:.3rem .4rem">&phi; (&deg;)</th>
        <th style="text-align:left;padding:.3rem .4rem">USCS</th><th style="text-align:right;padding:.3rem .4rem">FS</th><th style="text-align:left;padding:.3rem .4rem">Failure mode</th></tr>
      <tr><td style="padding:.25rem .4rem">S-1 Manikchari</td><td style="text-align:right">15</td><td style="text-align:right">70&deg;</td><td style="text-align:right">18.54</td><td style="text-align:right">1.3</td><td style="text-align:right">39.7</td><td>SM</td><td style="text-align:right;color:var(--rust)"><strong>0.90</strong></td><td>Shallow wedge</td></tr>
      <tr><td style="padding:.25rem .4rem">S-2 Manikchari S.</td><td style="text-align:right">10</td><td style="text-align:right">50&deg;</td><td style="text-align:right">19.02</td><td style="text-align:right">8.6</td><td style="text-align:right">37.3</td><td>SC</td><td style="text-align:right;color:var(--grn)"><strong>1.30</strong></td><td>Toe general</td></tr>
      <tr><td style="padding:.25rem .4rem">S-3 Shapchari</td><td style="text-align:right">10</td><td style="text-align:right">65&deg;</td><td style="text-align:right">18.98</td><td style="text-align:right">18.1</td><td style="text-align:right">10.3</td><td>CL</td><td style="text-align:right;color:var(--rust)"><strong>0.54</strong></td><td>Base failure</td></tr>
      <tr><td style="padding:.25rem .4rem">S-4 Deppoyachari</td><td style="text-align:right">16</td><td style="text-align:right">70&deg;</td><td style="text-align:right">19.00</td><td style="text-align:right">9.4</td><td style="text-align:right">27.4</td><td>CL-ML</td><td style="text-align:right;color:var(--rust)"><strong>0.86</strong></td><td>Toe general</td></tr>
      <tr><td style="padding:.25rem .4rem">S-5 Moddhapara</td><td style="text-align:right">13</td><td style="text-align:right">45&deg;</td><td style="text-align:right">19.34</td><td style="text-align:right">12.9</td><td style="text-align:right">32.7</td><td>CL</td><td style="text-align:right;color:var(--grn)"><strong>1.28</strong></td><td>Shallow surface</td></tr>
      <tr><td style="padding:.25rem .4rem">S-6 Kaching</td><td style="text-align:right">12</td><td style="text-align:right">50&deg;</td><td style="text-align:right">16.80</td><td style="text-align:right">1.8</td><td style="text-align:right">30.2</td><td>SM-SC</td><td style="text-align:right;color:var(--rust)"><strong>0.82</strong></td><td>Toe surficial</td></tr>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="small">Slope heights, angles and properties from Tables 1&ndash;2; factors of safety from the PLAXIS 2D Mohr&ndash;Coulomb analyses in Figure 9 of the paper.</p>

  <div class="grid3">
    <div class="vcard v-rust">
      <span class="vtag">Read S-3</span>
      <h4>Why the worst one was worst</h4>
      <p>FS = 0.54, the lowest of the six, and the only <strong>base failure</strong>. Look at the numbers: &phi; = 10.3&deg; is almost nothing, so the friction term is nearly absent &mdash; this slope stood on cohesion alone. Its natural moisture was 25.6% against a 27.7% saturation limit, so rain pushed it to saturation and softened the very cohesion holding it up.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="vcard v-amb">
      <span class="vtag">Read S-1 and S-6</span>
      <h4>Friction is no help when it's this steep</h4>
      <p>Both have high &phi; (39.7&deg; and 30.2&deg;) but almost no cohesion (1.3 and 1.8 kPa). Section 3 told you a cohesionless slope is stable only while &beta; &lt; &phi;'. At &beta; = 70&deg; and 50&deg;, both are far steeper than their friction angle &mdash; so with cohesion gone, failure is arithmetic.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="vcard v-grn">
      <span class="vtag">Read S-2 and S-5</span>
      <h4>The two that held</h4>
      <p>The only two with FS &gt; 1 are the two flattest slopes (50&deg; and 45&deg;), and both have meaningful cohesion <em>and</em> friction. S-2 also sat at 13.9% moisture against a 21.6% saturation limit &mdash; it had capacity to absorb rain before strength began dropping.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="why-matters">
    <span>&#9889;</span>
    <div><strong>The infiltration measurement.</strong> The authors ran a rainfall simulation on the S-2 soil: of 94 mm applied, 39 mm infiltrated and 55 mm ran off. That infiltration is the mechanism from Section 4 &mdash; rising moisture drives up pore pressure and drives down effective stress, exactly the &gamma;'/&gamma;<sub>sat</sub> collapse in Worked Example 2. The paper also notes the hill slopes largely had no drainage design, so water could not escape. Their recommended fixes are drainage and bioengineering with vegetation &mdash; both aimed squarely at keeping pore pressure down rather than making the soil stronger.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════ 13. QUICK REF + QUIZ ══════════════ -->
<section class="sec" id="sec-quiz">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">13</span><h2>Quick Reference &amp; Quick Check</h2></div>

  <div class="qref">
    <div><span>Factor of safety</span><span>FS = &tau;<sub>f</sub> / &tau;<sub>d</sub></span></div>
    <div><span>Infinite slope, dry</span><span>c'/(&gamma;Hcos&sup2;&beta;tan&beta;) + tan&phi;'/tan&beta;</span></div>
    <div><span>Infinite slope, seepage</span><span>c'/(&gamma;<sub>sat</sub>Hcos&sup2;&beta;tan&beta;) + (&gamma;'/&gamma;<sub>sat</sub>)(tan&phi;'/tan&beta;)</span></div>
    <div><span>Sand, dry (c'=0)</span><span>FS = tan&phi;'/tan&beta; (angle of repose)</span></div>
    <div><span>Critical height</span><span>H<sub>cr</sub> = c'/[&gamma;cos&sup2;&beta;(tan&beta;&minus;tan&phi;')]</span></div>
    <div><span>Taylor stability number</span><span>m = c'<sub>d</sub>/(&gamma;H); H<sub>cr</sub> = c'/(&gamma;m)</span></div>
    <div><span>Submerged unit weight</span><span>&gamma;' = &gamma;<sub>sat</sub> &minus; &gamma;<sub>w</sub></span></div>
    <div><span>Bishop m<sub>&alpha;</sub></span><span>cos&alpha; + sin&alpha;tan&phi;'/FS (iterate)</span></div>
    <div><span>Slice count</span><span>10&ndash;40 typical</span></div>
    <div><span>OMS vs Bishop</span><span>OMS conservative by 5&ndash;20%</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="quiz" id="quiz">
    <div class="q" data-a="1">
      <p>1. A dry sand slope (c' = 0) has &phi;' = 34&deg; and &beta; = 30&deg;. What happens to FS if the potential failure surface is 2 m deeper?</p>
      <button data-v="0">FS decreases &mdash; deeper means heavier</button>
      <button data-v="1">FS is unchanged &mdash; depth cancels when c' = 0</button>
      <button data-v="2">FS increases &mdash; more normal stress means more friction</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q" data-a="2">
      <p>2. Saturating a slope roughly halves the friction term. What is the physical reason?</p>
      <button data-v="0">Water lubricates the grains, lowering &phi;'</button>
      <button data-v="1">The soil becomes heavier, increasing driving stress</button>
      <button data-v="2">Pore water carries part of the load, cutting effective stress &mdash; and water has no shear strength</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q" data-a="0">
      <p>3. Why must slope analysis search many trial circles rather than compute just one?</p>
      <button data-v="0">Each circle gives the FS for that surface only; the slope fails on whichever is lowest</button>
      <button data-v="1">Because the method of slices is approximate and needs averaging</button>
      <button data-v="2">To check that the soil properties were measured correctly</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q" data-a="1">
      <p>4. Why does Bishop's Simplified Method require iteration when the Ordinary Method doesn't?</p>
      <button data-v="0">Because it uses more slices</button>
      <button data-v="1">FS appears on both sides of the equation, inside m<sub>&alpha;</sub>, so it can't be isolated</button>
      <button data-v="2">Because pore pressures are unknown in advance</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q" data-a="2">
      <p>5. Site S-3 at Chattogram had the highest cohesion of the six (18.1 kPa) yet the lowest FS (0.54). Why?</p>
      <button data-v="0">Its unit weight was the highest</button>
      <button data-v="1">It was the tallest slope</button>
      <button data-v="2">&phi; was only 10.3&deg;, so it stood almost entirely on cohesion &mdash; which rain then softened</button>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div id="quiz-score" class="quiz-score"></div>
</section>

